---

## Novel Insights

UniCon's most genuinely novel observation — that the pretrained backbone can serve as a *frozen feature pyramid* from which a fully trainable adapter reads, rather than as a network whose intermediate activations are modified — reframes adapter design from intervention to delegation. This framing naturally resolves the gradient overhead problem without approximation (unlike gradient checkpointing) and decouples adapter capacity from backbone architecture. The empirical observation (Table 1a) that decoder-focused adapters outperform encoder-focused ones for precision tasks (SR, deblur) in a pure transformer, while both halves contribute to high-level tasks, is a useful analytical result that extends the understanding of where control signal information is encoded in DiT models.

