## Novel Insights

The most genuinely novel insight emerging from this review synthesis (beyond the paper's explicit contributions) is the *discriminative power test for formal task classification*: the conjunction-wise additivity boundary rigorously separates transitive equivalence (impossible) from transitive ordering (possible) — two tasks long treated as equivalent in cognitive science. This formal separation grounds what has been largely an empirical debate, suggesting that the benchmark community may have been testing the wrong structural property when evaluating relational reasoning. The representational salience metric further offers a potential empirical tool for diagnosing kernel-regime models before they fail on compositional tasks, complementing the theoretical analysis.

