## Novel Insights

The paper's most genuinely novel insight — though underexploited in the analysis — is that the informational content flowing through the feedback channel (x(t) − x̂(t)) functions as an *online residual estimator*: the feedback loop effectively infers Δf(t) incrementally from state discrepancies without any explicit system identification step. This is closely analogous to the extended state observer principle from active disturbance rejection control, but applied inside the latent dynamics of a neural model rather than to physical plant outputs. The two-stage training strategy (freeze base model → train feedback via domain randomization) is a clean implementation of this insight and represents a reusable design pattern for any learned dynamical model where base accuracy must be preserved. The gap is that the paper does not fully exploit this framing: a deeper analysis of what the feedback network actually learns — and whether it approximates Δf(t) in an interpretable way — would substantially strengthen the contribution.

