## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Principled invariance test with formal guarantee**: Theorem 1 provides a clean theoretical criterion—if variance of P_+(X|Z) across different source priors is non-zero, then Z ≠ Pa[X]—which is then operationalized into the practical parent-finding criterion in Eq. (3). This gives the method a sound theoretical foundation that avoids heuristic scoring or restrictive parametric assumptions on the data-generating process.
2. **Quadratic complexity achieved via Markov blanket and sparsity exploitation**: Theorem 7 links candidate parent sets to maximal cliques in an augmented bidirectional graph over the Markov blanket, and combined with the empirically verified sparsity (p ≤ 13 across all benchmarks, Section 4.3), this yields O(d²) total complexity for graph recovery. The paper explicitly provides the complexity breakdown in Section 4.1 part D as O(md² + m|D| + m|B|).
3. **Strong empirical performance on large-scale real-world data**: On the Munin dataset (1041 variables), Table 2 shows GLIDE achieves 1.8% spurious rate compared to GIES's 42.36%, and produces results where PC cannot finish within 48 hours. This directly validates both accuracy and scalability on the largest benchmark.
4. **Versatility across data models**: Table 1 demonstrates GLIDE is the only method applicable to all four data model categories (L-G, nL-nG, categorical synthetic, categorical real-world), while all other baselines fail to produce meaningful results on at least one category.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Rigorous resampling procedure**: Theorems 4 and 5 provide a formal derivation of the optimal downsampling rate and the corresponding sampling-without-replacement procedure to create augmented datasets that match target source priors while preserving effect-cause conditionals. This avoids ad hoc data augmentation.
2. **Principled source prior selection**: Theorem 6 characterizes the convex hull C_r(γ₀) of admissible source priors on the r-dimensional simplex, enabling principled sampling of informative priors via Dirichlet sampling and K-means clustering (Section 4.2.4), rather than arbitrary choices.
3. **Scalability advantage empirically demonstrated in extreme settings**: In the extreme continuous setting (Figure 3, nL-nG), GLIDE achieves 9.6× and 25.52× faster processing than NOTEARS and MLP-NOTEARS while matching or outperforming them in SHD and spurious rate, directly supporting the claimed up to 25× speedup.

### Presentation / practical strengths
1. **Clear framework decomposition**: Figure 1 provides a well-structured overview separating the invariance test preparation (Part I) from the parent-finding search (Part II), making the two-phase pipeline immediately understandable.
2. **Public code availability**: The anonymous repository link (https://anonymous.4open.science/r/GLIDE-DC57) is provided for reproducibility.

## Summary

The paper introduces GLIDE, a causal graph learning framework that leverages the invariance of P(effect | cause) to changes in P(cause) to test for causal relationships via variance of conditional distributions across synthetically augmented datasets, combined with a quadratic-complexity parent search exploiting Markov blankets and graph sparsity. The single most important piece of evidence is the performance on the Munin dataset (Table 2), where GLIDE achieves 1.8% spurious rate on a 1041-variable graph—dramatically better than GIES's 42.36%—while producing results where the PC baseline fails to complete within 48 hours, simultaneously validating accuracy, reliability, and scalability.

