## Novel Insights

FDS offers an interesting observation: a pretrained optical flow model, even when applied to a blurry synthetic render ($C^s$), is more reliable for estimating pixel correspondences than the analytically-computed Radiance Flow from a poorly-optimized Gaussian scene. By routing the ground-truth input image $I^i$ through the matching model rather than the rendered $C^i$, the Prior Flow effectively circumvents floater artifacts in the rendered scene — turning a weakness of early-stage 3DGS (poor renders) into an advantage for the supervision signal. This "asymmetric" use of real and rendered images in the flow computation is a non-obvious design choice that the ablation (Table 4) confirms is critical.

---

